00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:43.230
But we do tend to all try to
follow the same general standard

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:44.510
when we're administering it.

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:48.579
So a lot of almost everything I'm saying
should be relevant to you as well.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:51.860
But make sure you do listen to
your professor exactly how they

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:54.709
instruct you for your video
or sorry for your practical.

00:00:55.099 --> 00:00:56.459
So let's go take a look at it.

00:00:56.459 --> 00:00:58.989
We're going to dive in exactly
to what to expect for this.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:00.230
What's the format of it?

00:01:00.519 --> 00:01:04.180
And then we're also, uh, whether it's
at the end of this video or in a future

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:08.130
video, we'll dive into the kind of the
major concepts of what to expect in each.

00:01:09.029 --> 00:01:09.810
So let's take a look.

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:20.049
All right, let's first talk about what
exactly should you expect when you

00:01:20.050 --> 00:01:22.330
walk into your laboratory practical.

00:01:22.759 --> 00:01:26.139
The first thing is, is generally
within a week before your practical,

00:01:26.139 --> 00:01:29.509
your instructor will assign a
time slot for you to show up.

00:01:29.939 --> 00:01:33.779
This activity is an individual
assignment, meaning it's testing

00:01:33.779 --> 00:01:35.819
you on your laboratory knowledge.

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:40.700
That you've accumulated over the course
over the entire semester, meaning that

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:42.090
will separate you into two groups.

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:45.850
So being that there are approximately
30 students in a lab section, there will

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:47.770
be 15 that will be in the first half.

00:01:48.225 --> 00:01:50.065
15 in the second half.

00:01:50.565 --> 00:01:51.985
Now, what does that mean?

00:01:51.985 --> 00:01:55.714
So when you assign your time,
you got to show up on time.

00:01:56.054 --> 00:02:00.054
So for example, let's say if my
class starts at 11am, that means

00:02:00.065 --> 00:02:01.734
half the students will be at 11am.

00:02:02.065 --> 00:02:04.995
The other half will be at
1230, which is the approximate

00:02:05.005 --> 00:02:06.714
halfway point for my section.

00:02:07.054 --> 00:02:09.035
So every section will
be a little different.

00:02:09.595 --> 00:02:12.475
So you must show up on time.

00:02:12.545 --> 00:02:13.805
That is crucial.

00:02:14.215 --> 00:02:19.940
Now, the big reason why is depending on
how fast The actual assignment or lab

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:22.300
practical will start if you show up late.

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:27.140
There's a big chance you will miss parts
of the practical that you cannot make up.

00:02:27.609 --> 00:02:31.469
Sometimes you might get lucky and then
maybe you could, you might, if you

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:34.729
miss a station, maybe you can make
it up along the way, but most of the

00:02:34.739 --> 00:02:38.010
time you are out of luck and you will
miss those points for your practical.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:41.190
Now, keep in mind when you show up.

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:45.460
So the second your instructor opens
the door and you're there, You need

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:50.750
to show up not only on time, but
already wearing your, your personal

00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:53.190
protective equipment, your PPE, right?

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:58.380
So, which is your lab coat, your
goggles, uh, pants, closed toed shoes.

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:02.119
You need to be wearing those
things ready to go into the

00:03:02.119 --> 00:03:03.650
room and to do your assignment.

00:03:04.019 --> 00:03:06.080
This can be a part of your grade as well.

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:08.960
There are many instructors
that will take off points.

00:03:09.309 --> 00:03:13.309
If you are missing any of these, because
remember this is testing you on your

00:03:13.309 --> 00:03:17.679
laboratory preparedness to see if
you know the proper lab skills before

00:03:17.679 --> 00:03:20.269
moving on to your next courses, right?

00:03:20.269 --> 00:03:22.910
So you need to show up
with those things in mind.

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:27.489
If you forget those things, you could
lose anywhere from, I've seen anywhere

00:03:27.489 --> 00:03:31.670
from 5 percent to as much as 50 percent
of the grade that could be deducted,

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:33.750
depending on who the instructor is.

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:37.260
Some instructors will even send you
home, and then you're just out of luck.

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:38.580
So keep that in mind.

00:03:38.659 --> 00:03:40.589
You want to make sure you don't do that.

00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:44.060
If you have to the night before, put
some extra clothes in your car if you

00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:45.880
have to, so that way you have them.

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:49.019
Alright, the other thing is, not to
freak you out, but it's just because it's

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:50.600
serious, right, when you're doing this.

00:03:51.035 --> 00:03:55.924
Is you want to, in your lab coat pocket,
put a, your calculator and put a pen or

00:03:55.954 --> 00:03:59.454
pencil as well to some, whatever you want
to write with throughout the activity.

00:03:59.724 --> 00:04:02.135
So that way you have that ready to go.

00:04:02.744 --> 00:04:06.384
Now when you come in, the next
thing is your instructor is going

00:04:06.384 --> 00:04:08.524
to let all of you in one at a time.

00:04:08.865 --> 00:04:11.145
And assign you a starting position.

00:04:11.365 --> 00:04:13.855
It's a, it's what we
refer to as a station.

00:04:14.175 --> 00:04:17.274
So you're going to see essentially
stations all around the room.

00:04:17.285 --> 00:04:19.024
It's going to go from one through ten.

00:04:19.425 --> 00:04:21.544
And there's going to be all these
different spots that are going to tell

00:04:21.545 --> 00:04:23.125
you is where you're going to start.

00:04:23.625 --> 00:04:27.094
So the first thing you do when you
come in, you'll put your backpack

00:04:27.185 --> 00:04:30.615
at the front of the room with your
phone, um, any bags you might have.

00:04:30.955 --> 00:04:33.735
Anything that essentially that
you can't use on the exam,

00:04:34.015 --> 00:04:34.985
they'll all go by the front.

00:04:35.645 --> 00:04:38.395
Then you're going to go to your
station, which then you're just going

00:04:38.395 --> 00:04:42.114
to kind of wait and until the start
of the start of the actual session.

00:04:42.484 --> 00:04:43.434
So just go there.

00:04:43.444 --> 00:04:44.974
Do not touch anything.

00:04:45.215 --> 00:04:46.504
Don't talk to anybody.

00:04:46.504 --> 00:04:48.194
Just stand there ready to go.

00:04:48.484 --> 00:04:50.405
It's supposed to be a very serious thing.

00:04:50.405 --> 00:04:50.715
So.

00:04:50.965 --> 00:04:54.265
When you're there, just be ready
to work when essentially your

00:04:54.265 --> 00:04:55.935
instructor tells you further.

00:04:56.365 --> 00:04:56.765
Okay.

00:04:57.215 --> 00:05:02.044
So now from there, the way that this
part can work, it depends on how,

00:05:02.055 --> 00:05:03.724
when they hand out the versions.

00:05:04.074 --> 00:05:06.954
Um, so there are three different
lab practical versions.

00:05:06.955 --> 00:05:10.834
You might just get the version when
you walk in, other instructors may

00:05:10.854 --> 00:05:15.975
go around the room, handing out a
different color coded answer sheet.

00:05:16.385 --> 00:05:19.525
For you to fill out all of your
info, all this, all the, uh,

00:05:19.535 --> 00:05:21.485
station info, all your answers on.

00:05:21.935 --> 00:05:25.835
And that's going to be the version you're
going to be following the entire time.

00:05:26.234 --> 00:05:28.075
So you can get A, B, or C.

00:05:28.075 --> 00:05:29.145
They are different colors.

00:05:29.615 --> 00:05:33.354
Um, so for example, let's say if I
have B, what I'm going to be doing

00:05:33.354 --> 00:05:37.844
is every time I'm at a station, I'm
going to be answering question for

00:05:37.854 --> 00:05:40.664
version B at each station as I go.

00:05:40.955 --> 00:05:42.535
So you ignore the other versions.

00:05:42.535 --> 00:05:43.505
They don't apply to you.

00:05:43.745 --> 00:05:47.445
You're doing your own practical,
uh, throughout the entire thing.

00:05:47.455 --> 00:05:50.165
So there's going to be multiple different
versions and everybody's going to have

00:05:50.165 --> 00:05:51.654
their own, essentially their own tests.

00:05:52.265 --> 00:05:56.205
So now when you get there with
that version, you got to keep in

00:05:56.205 --> 00:05:59.304
mind that when the timers, when
everybody's in place, there will be

00:05:59.304 --> 00:06:01.434
a timer set at the front of the room.

00:06:01.734 --> 00:06:06.825
Usually it's shown on the projector screen
where you're always given four minutes

00:06:06.865 --> 00:06:12.165
to complete the And then one minute
afterwards, that's like a one minute

00:06:12.165 --> 00:06:18.044
warning of after the four minutes to
clean up, to switch stations, to wrap up.

00:06:18.325 --> 00:06:22.514
So by the end of the one minute, you
should already be standing or ready

00:06:22.514 --> 00:06:24.645
to go at the next station pretty much.

00:06:25.035 --> 00:06:26.454
So keep that in mind.

00:06:26.845 --> 00:06:30.245
Overall, you have about five minutes
to do whatever it is they need to do.

00:06:30.665 --> 00:06:33.825
So when we talk about what
to expect at each station, it

00:06:33.825 --> 00:06:35.415
has to be something doable.

00:06:35.835 --> 00:06:38.525
In that first four minutes,
there has to be something faster.

00:06:38.785 --> 00:06:41.535
So don't try to study all
these crazy things that are

00:06:41.535 --> 00:06:42.845
going to take you hours to do.

00:06:42.855 --> 00:06:44.785
It has to be doable in a few minutes.

00:06:46.004 --> 00:06:48.205
Now from there, let's go
look on to our next part.

00:06:48.355 --> 00:06:52.674
So let's talk about how the entire
process works once that timer starts.

00:06:53.084 --> 00:06:54.675
So the time has started.

00:06:54.715 --> 00:06:57.585
What you're going to be doing is you're
going to just answer your version.

00:06:57.969 --> 00:07:02.299
So if I had blue, I'm going to be
answering version B for my station.

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:06.020
So when you're doing this, keep in
mind, the whole station is something

00:07:06.020 --> 00:07:08.619
that's doable in four to five minutes.

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:12.950
So when you're studying, you want to
try to be studying concepts of things

00:07:12.950 --> 00:07:15.169
that are manageable in that timeframe.

00:07:15.449 --> 00:07:18.829
You're not going to be asked to do
an entire experiment in four minutes.

00:07:18.829 --> 00:07:19.979
That's not possible.

00:07:20.430 --> 00:07:22.860
So when you're looking at
something that's doable.

00:07:23.075 --> 00:07:25.985
It's designed to be finished
in that time period.

00:07:26.365 --> 00:07:30.295
So if you are someone that's running
out of time, one thing to keep in mind

00:07:30.295 --> 00:07:33.985
is just write down the question because
usually you're, you're always given time

00:07:33.985 --> 00:07:37.234
at the end to work up your question.

00:07:37.784 --> 00:07:41.164
So if, let's say when I got,
when I came into the room, I

00:07:41.164 --> 00:07:42.825
was in my lab coat ready to go.

00:07:43.094 --> 00:07:46.414
And I was told by my instructor,
you're going to station six.

00:07:46.965 --> 00:07:50.455
So what you're going to do at
station six, when the timer comes,

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:52.870
You'll flip over your station card.

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:54.270
It will have the question.

00:07:54.660 --> 00:07:59.360
You're going to answer question
six in the number six answer box

00:07:59.390 --> 00:08:01.050
on your, on your version sheet.

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:04.269
So it's gonna have one, two, three,
four, five, six, all the way to 10.

00:08:04.585 --> 00:08:06.035
You will start on number six.

00:08:06.635 --> 00:08:09.855
Now, when you're answering that,
the thing to keep in mind is none

00:08:09.855 --> 00:08:11.395
of these are multiple choice.

00:08:11.425 --> 00:08:14.975
There are things that you should have
mastered or understood by this point.

00:08:15.375 --> 00:08:17.864
Um, or at least ask your
instructor if there's something

00:08:17.864 --> 00:08:19.264
that you need further help with.

00:08:19.715 --> 00:08:25.274
So all of it is free response questions,
meaning their, their calculations, maybe

00:08:25.294 --> 00:08:27.544
balancing, maybe measuring something.

00:08:27.854 --> 00:08:30.304
They're all things that you've
been working on throughout

00:08:30.304 --> 00:08:32.105
the as you work up your labs.

00:08:32.845 --> 00:08:38.695
Now, when the timer goes up, that is
when you'll rotate to the next station.

00:08:39.025 --> 00:08:41.595
And typically the way these are
working is that they're always

00:08:41.605 --> 00:08:44.635
in increasing order as you go.

00:08:44.985 --> 00:08:48.995
So if you start at six, you will
then rotate to number seven.

00:08:49.465 --> 00:08:53.255
Now the nice thing is, is you're always
going to be following the same person.

00:08:53.265 --> 00:08:56.925
So there's going to be another student
at, let's say, the station next to you.

00:08:57.515 --> 00:08:59.165
They're going to be going in front of you.

00:08:59.165 --> 00:09:00.814
So you're always going to
follow the same person.

00:09:00.814 --> 00:09:01.755
So if I'm at six.

00:09:02.119 --> 00:09:04.729
I'm going to follow the student
at number seven the entire way

00:09:04.729 --> 00:09:06.479
through as it's increasing.

00:09:07.650 --> 00:09:11.470
Now there might be also depending
on how big your group is.

00:09:11.810 --> 00:09:14.840
So being that there's only what,
10 stations that are there,

00:09:15.080 --> 00:09:18.469
well, if you remember, I said
there could be up to 15 students.

00:09:18.859 --> 00:09:22.639
What that means is that there's going
to be something called a rest stop

00:09:22.699 --> 00:09:27.869
at different points around the class,
meaning that between certain stations,

00:09:27.869 --> 00:09:31.069
there might be a, might be an area
where you can kind of just relax.

00:09:31.395 --> 00:09:36.405
For the five minute window of opportunity,
or you can finish up other questions,

00:09:36.695 --> 00:09:39.295
so you might see that there will
be some of them and your instructor

00:09:39.295 --> 00:09:42.105
will notify you if that's something
they are doing for that session.

00:09:42.434 --> 00:09:43.665
Every time it is different.

00:09:43.665 --> 00:09:47.005
It depends on whether or not depends
on the number of students in your time.

00:09:47.770 --> 00:09:51.530
So at the end of what you're going to
be doing, so you'll do all 10 stations.

00:09:51.860 --> 00:09:55.010
Uh, generally this takes about
an hour and a half or so to

00:09:55.010 --> 00:09:56.300
hour to an hour and a half.

00:09:56.849 --> 00:10:00.519
Um, when you're done, you're usually
always given about five minutes

00:10:00.520 --> 00:10:04.369
at the end, just to wrap up your
questions, maybe answer something

00:10:04.369 --> 00:10:06.059
if you didn't quite finish it.

00:10:06.499 --> 00:10:11.110
Um, just to really, just to make sure
you get as many points as possible.

00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:13.370
to pass your lab practical.

00:10:13.710 --> 00:10:15.870
So keep that in mind
as you are doing that.

00:10:16.110 --> 00:10:17.850
So let's go look at the next part.

00:10:18.110 --> 00:10:21.160
So what can we test on
in this entire thing?

00:10:21.500 --> 00:10:24.179
Well, there are many things that
we can always test you on when it

00:10:24.179 --> 00:10:26.940
comes to chemistry, because as you
kind of learned about this semester,

00:10:27.230 --> 00:10:28.690
there's a ton of information.

00:10:28.699 --> 00:10:31.960
It is a lot that goes into
introductory chemistry.

00:10:32.540 --> 00:10:35.270
Um, so the first thing is, did
you show up properly in lab?

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:35.530
Right?

00:10:35.530 --> 00:10:39.260
So wearing that proper PPE and wearing
your lab coat, goggles, all that stuff.

00:10:39.555 --> 00:10:43.885
Is one of the first things, you know, does
that tell me that you are that you are

00:10:43.885 --> 00:10:46.765
essentially treat lab as a professional
environment because you want to be a

00:10:46.765 --> 00:10:50.165
professional one day in your life as well,
where you're either a health care worker,

00:10:50.375 --> 00:10:54.335
a scientist or whatever it is, you've
got to wear proper PPE in your career.

00:10:54.654 --> 00:10:57.064
So this is something that you
need to be tested on, right?

00:10:57.214 --> 00:10:58.205
Make sure you can do that.

00:10:58.485 --> 00:11:01.705
The next thing is really, can you
measure, can you do measurements?

00:11:02.050 --> 00:11:05.579
One of the most crucial things that
you need to get out of this class

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:11.100
is can you do measurements because
in every career, especially in STEM

00:11:11.300 --> 00:11:14.800
or the health sciences, measurements
are the backbone for what we do.

00:11:15.159 --> 00:11:18.249
And in many cases, if you don't do
measurements correctly, it can lead

00:11:18.249 --> 00:11:22.170
to a lot of different problems in a
lot of different careers, whether it's

00:11:22.180 --> 00:11:25.659
lawsuits or maybe even worse, where
someone could get injured or hurt.

00:11:26.030 --> 00:11:27.949
So you need to be able
to do the basics, right?

00:11:28.455 --> 00:11:32.205
So the first thing is you have to
write down everything you're doing

00:11:32.205 --> 00:11:33.475
when you're at these stations.

00:11:33.845 --> 00:11:37.414
A lot of times there can be, there's
partial credit that can be awarded at

00:11:37.414 --> 00:11:41.735
every station, especially when it comes
to showing your work, writing your

00:11:41.735 --> 00:11:46.035
calculations down, showing that you, that
you know something about the problem.

00:11:46.394 --> 00:11:50.655
So write down all of your units, write
down all of your significant figures.

00:11:51.055 --> 00:11:56.515
Know the basic idea of how to measure
things like tick marks on glassware and

00:11:56.515 --> 00:12:00.194
how to actually approximate the number of
sig figs when you're going through them.

00:12:00.665 --> 00:12:02.944
Um, so those are going to
be really important, right?

00:12:02.955 --> 00:12:06.534
Sig figs have been drilled into
all of you every single day

00:12:06.534 --> 00:12:07.704
in every homework assignment.

00:12:08.085 --> 00:12:10.295
Of course, it's going to be
a part of the lab practical.

00:12:10.295 --> 00:12:11.145
That doesn't go away.

00:12:11.395 --> 00:12:15.375
Measurements have to be precise
and need to be accurate as well.

00:12:15.735 --> 00:12:16.935
So keep that in mind.

00:12:17.824 --> 00:12:19.275
Now the next part is equipment.

00:12:19.744 --> 00:12:23.244
Can you name basic glassware
that you use in your labs?

00:12:23.675 --> 00:12:25.914
This is the easy point questions, right?

00:12:25.914 --> 00:12:29.874
You should know what a beaker is,
what an Erlenmeyer flask is, what

00:12:29.874 --> 00:12:33.214
a thermometer is, what a pipette
is, what a volumetric flask.

00:12:33.525 --> 00:12:36.464
If some of these names are things
that you don't recognize, then I would

00:12:36.464 --> 00:12:38.754
recommend reviewing your glassware.

00:12:39.024 --> 00:12:42.024
There's a couple ways you can
do this depending on how your

00:12:42.024 --> 00:12:43.014
teacher set up your course.

00:12:43.525 --> 00:12:46.695
You might, the one thing is that
there's probably a study guide in

00:12:46.695 --> 00:12:50.665
